 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Training Failure:** Removing the projection loss causes training to fail entirely, as nothing ties an anchor's Gaussians to the image region it was encoded from [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Neighbor Retrieval:** Removing the Voronoi loss causes a significant PSNR drop (2.51 dB) because Gaussians may leave their anchor's cell, and the hierarchical search would no longer retrieve their true neighbors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Local Consistency:** Removing the local refinement module lowers PSNR by 0.96 dB, as each Gaussian is predicted from its own token alone and cannot be made consistent with its neighbors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Budget Constraint:** The allocation policy must strictly enforce $\sum k_n = B$. The use of GSPO with multinomial sampling ensures this constraint is met during training [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Allocation Sum Constraint:** The allocation policy must strictly satisfy $\sum k_n = B$. Failure to do so breaks the resource-linear guarantee [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Projection Loss:** Removing the projection loss causes training to fail entirely, as it ties anchor Gaussians to their image regions [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Voronoi Loss:** Removing the Voronoi loss causes a significant PSNR drop (2.51 dB) because Gaussians leave their anchor's cell, and the hierarchical search retrieves incorrect neighbors [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Local Refinement:** Removing the local refinement module lowers PSNR by 0.96 dB, as Gaussians cannot be made consistent with neighbors [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Hierarchical neighbor search is 27× faster than exact search but may introduce approximation errors in neighbor identification [S1, unofficial].
